#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151899 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "125ed0b3-14ad-5af8-8370-ca6f83d93c35" :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase"}] :question/id #uuid "2cfd1ed7-8249-5970-9ddc-1bb69929951c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21450" :question/problem "What number is $40\\%$ greater than $360$?" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $40\\%$ *greater than* $360$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140\\% = \\dfrac{140}{100} = 1.40\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $40\\%$ greater than $360$, we multiply $360$ by $1.40{:}$\n\n$$\n1.40 \\cdot 360 = 504\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $40\\%$ greater than $360$ is $504$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151900 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f90ea994-79f2-5409-bf96-306803b4ef01" :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "95c3dedd-accd-57dc-91ee-4ee8fdb2f3a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21451" :question/problem "A t-shirt costs $\\[math]18$ before tax. If a $9\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total cost after tax?" :question/worked-solution "To find the total cost after tax, we apply a $9\\%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $9\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 9\\% = 109\\% = 1.09\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.09{:}$\n\n$$\n18 \\times 1.09 = 19.62\n$$\n\nSo, the total cost after tax is $\\[math]19.62$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151901 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88d0f4ad-a3f9-5275-b5d5-a9973bf63ebf" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"}] :question/id #uuid "4af9bc31-4980-5c3c-9025-45e85ac09d8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21517" :question/problem "A number $x$ is $150\\%$ greater than $20$. Find the value of $x$." :question/worked-solution "To find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $20$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting our percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $20$, we multiply $20$ by $2.5{:}$\n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 20 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = {50}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb0103d-d686-575a-8ef8-81640227f4dc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Word Problems Involving Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"}] :question/id #uuid "92d9c3ca-6e59-5c73-b640-114dfb7d13cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-22093" :question/problem "A gym had $80$ members last year. This year, membership is $400\\%$ greater than last year. How many members does the gym have this year?" :question/worked-solution "To find this year's gym membership, we apply a $400\\%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 400\\% = 500\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500\\% = \\dfrac{500}{100} = 5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n80 \\times 5 = 400\n$$\n\nSo, the gym has $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{400}$ members this year."}]}
